nt not in architecture context → generate onboarding chain: `repo-onboarding` + `license-validation` (parallel start) → `konflux-onboarding` (after repo-onboarding) → general implementation (after license-validation; parallel with konflux-onboarding). For new container image in existing repo: skip repo-onboarding, start with image build Implementation + `konflux-onboarding` (parallel).\n147:24. `docs-authoring` epic → add \"technically reviewed against implementation\" AC\n172:| `docs-authoring` | Red Hat docs content process — specialized tooling |\n175:| `repo-onboarding` | Midstream repo fork creation under opendatahub-io |\n298:   The four section headings are literal and required for EVERY epic, including `docs-authoring` epics — an outline of content topics goes INSIDE the `## Scope` section, never as a renamed `## Content Outline` heading (a renamed heading fails the artifact contract).\n356:It deterministically checks what downstream consumers enforce and an LLM step can silently get wrong: epic body sections (Description / Scope / Acceptance Criteria / HLR Traceability), `component` values verbatim in `.context/rhai-components.txt`, Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by every Implementation epic), priority inheritance (an epic blocking P0 work is itself P0), and implementation-type parallelism (Rules 7/8/10 — no `dependencies` edge, in either direction, between `license-validation` and `repo-onboarding`, `license-validation` and `konflux-onboarding`, or `konflux-onboarding` and a general implementation; a flagged edge is removed from the dependent epic's `dependencies` and its coupling expressed as an AC gate instead). It prints one `VIOLATION:` line per problem and exits non-zero. Fix every violation by editing the epic files (never edit the script), re-run, and proceed only once it prints `OK`.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9881939f56c42020","agent_id":"a4ac3513da74bdce4","session_id":"0ac6dbf8-8647-4b18-86ad-04e38ef35119","uuid":"